WEBVTT

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:36.190
<v Ira Glass>Hey, there, America. Can we just get this election over with already? We've had enough excitement, right? More than enough plot twists. The president getting coronavirus, Ruth Bader Ginsburg dying, that whole first debate, the alleged contents of Hunter Biden's hard drive-- which are either totally unconvincing or deeply, urgently important, depending on your point of view.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，美国。我们能快点结束这场选举吗？我们经历的刺激已经够多了，对吧？情节反转也已经足够多了。总统感染了冠状病毒，露丝·巴德·金斯伯格去世，那场首场辩论，还有亨特·拜登硬盘里的所谓内容——根据你的观点，这些内容要么完全令人难以信服，要么极其重要且紧迫。

00:00:36.190 --> 00:01:04.450
<v Ira Glass>We finally got to see the president's tax records after all these years. That barely seemed to make a dent in anything. Militia plotted to kidnap the governor of Michigan. And then after that, the president's followers chanted, "Lock her up." The president joking, not joking, told his supporters in North Carolina to go out and try to vote twice. And then after that, the attorney general declared on CNN to Wolf Blitzer that voting twice might actually be legal.
<v Ira Glass>这些年来，我们终于看到了总统的税务记录。但这似乎几乎没有产生任何影响。民兵密谋绑架密歇根州州长。此后，总统的支持者高呼“把她关起来”。总统在北卡罗来纳州告诉他的支持者去试着投两次票，是在开玩笑，还是没开玩笑？之后，司法部长在 CNN 对沃尔夫·布利策宣称，投两次票实际上可能是合法的。

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.460
<v William Barr>I don't know what the law in that particular state says.
<v William Barr>我不知道那个特定州的法律是怎么说的。

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.260
<v Wolf Blitzer>You can't vote twice.
<v Wolf Blitzer>你不能投两次票。

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.980
<v William Barr>Well, I don't know what the law in that particular state says. And when that vote becomes final--
<v William Barr>嗯，我不知道那个特定州的法律是怎么说的。当那张选票最终生效时——

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.960
<v Wolf Blitzer>Is there any state that says you could vote twice?
<v Wolf Blitzer>有哪个州说你可以投两次票吗？

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:20.220
<v William Barr>Well, there's some maybe that you can change your vote up to a particular time. I don't know what the law is, so I'm not going to offer.
<v William Barr>嗯，也许有些州你可以更改你的选票，直到某个时间点。我不知道法律是什么，所以我不会提供建议。

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:33.240
<v Ira Glass>Attorney General William Barr looks amazingly relaxed as he says all this. Calm, like nothing's out of the ordinary. Nothing to see here, officer. Watching him, it's hard not to think, is this really happening?
<v Ira Glass>司法部长威廉·巴尔在说这一切时看起来非常放松。很冷静，好像没什么不寻常的。警察同志，这里没什么可看的。看着他，很难不让人想，这真的在发生吗？

00:01:33.240 --> 00:02:09.420
<v Ira Glass>I got that same feeling looking at photos of the president's rallies where, even now, he's packing people in. Tons of people with no masks, not following CDC guidelines. He's doing this even after he, his wife, his son, his press secretary, his campaign manager, the chair of the Republican National Committee, White House housekeeping and military staff, members of the vice president's staff, Stephen Miller, Kellyanne Conway, and Hope Hicks all got coronavirus. Even though cases are at an all-time high and on the rise. Looking at the not socially distanced crowds, it's like, is this really happening?
<v Ira Glass>看着总统集会的照片，我也有同样的感觉，即使现在，他仍然挤满了人。成吨的人没有戴口罩，没有遵循疾控中心的指导方针。即使在他本人、他的妻子、他的儿子、他的新闻秘书、他的竞选经理、共和党全国委员会主席、白宫管家和军事人员、副总统的工作人员、斯蒂芬·米勒、凯莉安·康威和霍普·希克斯都感染了冠状病毒之后，他仍然在这样做。即使病例处于历史最高水平且在不断上升。看着那些没有保持社交距离的人群，就像是，这真的在发生吗？

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:35.120
<v Ira Glass>And now, coming up, this coming Tuesday, we're either going to have an election with a clear winner or, apparently, the vote will be contested. A contested election seems so possible because the president keeps saying over and over that he's way ahead in the polls, and those polls that we are always seeing on the news saying that he's way behind, those are fake. And so if he loses, he says, there's only one possible explanation.
<v Ira Glass>现在，即将到来的下周二，我们要么会迎来一场有明确获胜者的选举，要么显然，投票结果将被质疑。一场有争议的选举似乎很有可能，因为总统反复说他在民调中遥遥领先，而我们在新闻中经常看到那些说他远远落后的民调是假的。所以如果他输了，他说，只有一个可能的解释。

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:46.370
<v Donald Trump>The only way we can lose, in my opinion, is massive fraud. And that's what's happening, because all over the country, you're seeing it, thousands and thousands of ballots.
<v Donald Trump>在我看来，我们输掉选举的唯一途径就是大规模欺诈。这就是正在发生的事情，因为在全国各地，你都能看到成千上万张选票。

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.400
<v Ira Glass>That was Monday in Allentown. Here he is in Oshkosh, Wisconsin.
<v Ira Glass>那是周一在阿伦敦。这是他在威斯康星州奥什科什。

00:02:50.400 --> 00:03:01.320
<v Donald Trump>Because the only way we're going to lose this election is if the election is rigged. Remember that. It's the only way we're going to lose this election. And we can't let that happen.
<v Donald Trump>因为我们输掉这次选举的唯一途径就是选举被操纵了。记住这一点。这是我们输掉这次选举的唯一途径。我们不能让这种情况发生。

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:18.620
<v Ira Glass>There is no evidence for any massive fraud like he's talking about. When he talks about mail-in ballots being a problem-- which he does nearly everywhere-- there's no evidence for that either. Lots more Democrats than Republicans are voting with mail-in ballots this year. Here's the president on Sunday in New Hampshire, calling those ballots into question.
<v Ira Glass>没有任何证据表明他所说的那种大规模欺诈存在。当他谈到邮寄选票是个问题时——他几乎在到处都这么说——对此也没有任何证据。今年通过邮寄选票投票的民主党人比共和党人多得多。这是总统周日在新罕布什尔州对这些选票提出质疑。

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:35.290
<v Donald Trump>The other way, they said millions of ballots. We don't know who's sending them. We don't know where they're going. We don't know who's bringing them back. We don't know who's signed them. And it shouldn't be allowed. And the Democrats know it's a hoax. And we shouldn't let it happen.
<v Donald Trump>另一种方式，他们说有数百万张选票。我们不知道是谁寄出的。我们不知道它们要去哪里。我们不知道是谁带回来的。我们不知道是谁签名的。这不应该被允许。民主党人知道这是一个骗局。我们不应该让它发生。

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:57.690
<v Ira Glass>So, this Tuesday, he may fight those ballots. And we might find ourselves in a big, no-holds-barred season finale, contested election, constitutional national meltdown, with both sides arguing over whether to count mail-in ballots and ballots that arrive after November 3. And if that happens, people on both sides will probably take to the streets to protest, and who knows where that might lead?
<v Ira Glass>所以，这个周二，他可能会对这些选票提出抗议。我们可能会发现自己处于一场大型、肆无忌惮的季终集，一场有争议的选举，一场宪法级的国家崩溃，双方都在争论是否要清点邮寄选票和 11 月 3 日之后送达的选票。如果发生这种情况，双方的人可能都会走上街头抗议，谁知道这会导致什么结果呢？

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:15.720
<v Ira Glass>And then, of course, there are the scenarios where the whole thing gets thrown to Congress or the Supreme Court to decide. So many different ways this could play out, where, whichever side loses, red or blue, they'll probably never be able to see the final decision as fair and legitimate. So one half of the country will not accept who the other half puts into office.
<v Ira Glass>然后，当然，还有整个事件被提交给国会或最高法院来裁决的情况。这事可能会以多种方式发展，无论哪一方输了，红色还是蓝色，他们可能永远无法将最终决定视为公平和合法的。所以国家的一半人将不会接受另一半人选出的人进入办公室。

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:45.870
<v Ira Glass>Or maybe that won't happen. Maybe somebody is going to win decisively. That's where we are. Our world is either about to slip into utter chaos or the election will go just fine, and things will be fine. That's the unreality of now. There are so many things right now that make it feel like the ground underneath us is thin as a cracker with molten lava underneath. So many things, it's hard to understand. This is real. This is really happening.
<v Ira Glass>或者也许那不会发生。也许有人会决定性地获胜。这就是我们目前的处境。我们的世界要么即将陷入彻底的混乱，要么选举会进展顺利，一切都会好起来。这就是当下的非现实感。现在有太多的事情让人觉得我们脚下的地面薄得像饼干，下面流淌着熔岩。太多事情了，让人难以理解。这是真实的。这真的在发生。

00:04:45.870 --> 00:05:09.010
<v Ira Glass>And today on our program, we have people trying to get their minds around that. I have to say that is so much of the mood as we head into the Simpsons Halloween Special of an election day. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们邀请了试图理解这一切的人。我不得不说，在我们走向辛普森一家选举日万圣节特别节目时，这就是大部分的情绪。来自芝加哥 WBEZ，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:37.200
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Would You Like to Come Up to First Class? So the journalist E. Jean Carroll has been in the news this week when a federal judge ruled that the president can be sued by Caroll. Caroll says that back in the 1990s, Donald Trump raped her in a dressing room in the department store at Bergdorf Goodman. He says she's lying. She's suing him for defamation. She's one of dozens of women who've accused the president of sexual assault and harassment. The president denies all of their allegations.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，你想上头等舱吗？记者 E·珍·卡罗尔本周上了新闻，一位联邦法官裁定，卡罗尔可以起诉总统。卡罗尔说，在 20 世纪 90 年代，唐纳德·特朗普在波道夫·古德曼百货公司的试衣间里强奸了她。他说她在撒谎。她以诽谤罪起诉他。她是几十位指控总统性侵犯和骚扰的女性之一。总统否认了她们所有的指控。

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:55.810
<v Ira Glass>These stories have been so widely covered and everybody is so used to them. But to Caroll, it felt like, at this point, they were just being ignored, which seemed kind of incredible to her. Our program today is about people feeling like, is this is really happening? And she definitely felt that about this. It killed her that these stories just somehow didn't seem to matter.
<v Ira Glass>这些故事被广泛报道，每个人都已经习惯了。但对卡罗尔来说，感觉就像，在这一点上，它们只是被忽视了，这对她来说似乎有点令人难以置信。我们今天的节目是关于那些感觉像“这真的在发生吗？”的人。她绝对对这一点有这种感觉。这些故事不知何故似乎并不重要，这让她感到非常痛苦。

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:18.750
<v Ira Glass>And this summer, she decided to try to do something about it. She had a bunch of conversations with other women who have accused the president and published them in a series of stories in The Atlantic. And her idea was, most people only have a vague sense of these stories and don't have much of a sense of what these women say actually happened. And by diving into the details of these stories, she'd restore them to human size, in full color.
<v Ira Glass>今年夏天，她决定尝试对此做点什么。她与指控总统的其他女性进行了多次谈话，并将它们发表在《大西洋月刊》的一系列故事中。她的想法是，大多数人对这些故事只有模糊的印象，对这些女性说实际发生了什么并没有太多的了解。通过深入挖掘这些故事的细节，她将把它们恢复到真实的人类尺度，全彩呈现。

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:31.980
<v Ira Glass>She's adapted one of the articles that she wrote for us. It's with one of the president's accusers named Jessica Leeds. A warning about content before we start-- this is a frank conversation about sexual assault. Here's E. Jean Caroll.
<v Ira Glass>她改编了她为我们写的一篇文章。对象是总统的一位指控者杰西卡·利兹。开始前关于内容的警告——这是一场关于性侵犯的坦诚谈话。这是 E·珍·卡罗尔。

00:06:31.980 --> 00:07:00.070
<v E. Jean Carroll>Let me set the scene-- midsummer twilight, and Jessica Leeds and I are letting down what's left of our hair. Jessica's in her elegant crib in the Blue Ridge mountains. I'm in my little shack just off the Appalachian Trail. And we're Zooming like two old screech owls, discovering how much we had in common, which includes our love for Mr. Paul Newman, our age, and our height.
<v E. Jean Carroll>让我来设定场景——盛夏黄昏，杰西卡·利兹和我正在放下我们剩余的头发。杰西卡在蓝岭山脉她优雅的住所里。我在阿巴拉契亚小径旁边的小屋里。我们像两只老尖叫猫头鹰一样在 Zoom 上交谈，发现我们有这么多共同点，包括我们对保罗·纽曼先生的热爱，我们的年龄和我们的身高。

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:03.180
<v E. Jean Carroll>How tall are you now?
<v E. Jean Carroll>你现在多高？

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:09.240
<v Jessica Leeds>Well, I'm 5' 7" now. I was 5' 9" at the-- I've lost two inches.
<v Jessica Leeds>嗯，我现在 5 英尺 7 英寸。我曾经是 5 英尺 9 英寸——我瘦了 2 英寸。

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.090
<v E. Jean Carroll>Me, too. I've lost two inches.
<v E. Jean Carroll>我也是。我瘦了 2 英寸。

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:20.940
<v Jessica Leeds>I don't know where they've gone, but I've lost them. I love being tall. When I was working, I was always so thankful--
<v Jessica Leeds>我不知道它们去哪了，但我确实瘦了。我喜欢个子高。当我工作时，我总是非常感谢——

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.570
<v E. Jean Carroll>Yeah, me, too.
<v E. Jean Carroll>是的，我也是。

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.190
<v Jessica Leeds>--that I was tall.
<v Jessica Leeds>——我个子高。

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:36.820
<v E. Jean Carroll>About 40 years ago, when Jessica was working as a salesperson for a company that sold newsprint to publications like the Washington Post, she sat down next to Donald Trump on an airplane.
<v E. Jean Carroll>大约 40 年前，当杰西卡作为一家为《华盛顿邮报》等出版物销售新闻纸的公司的销售员工作时，她在飞机上坐在了唐纳德·特朗普旁边。

00:07:36.820 --> 00:08:05.550
<v E. Jean Carroll>Let me just say that if you have never Zoomed with a silver-haired, soigné 78-year-old woman who describes what it's like being strapped in a seat on a Braniff Airlines flight with a future president of the United States trying to fasten his lips on her like a 6' 2" suckfish-- well, in my opinion, you have not lived, let alone Zoomed, at all.
<v E. Jean Carroll>让我说一句，如果你从未与一位银发、优雅的 78 岁女性进行过 Zoom 交谈，她描述了被束缚在布兰尼夫航空公司的航班座位上，一位未来的美国总统试图像 6 英尺 2 英寸的吸鱼一样把嘴唇贴在她身上是什么感觉——嗯，在我看来，你根本没有活过，更不用说在 Zoom 上谈话了。

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:20.160
<v E. Jean Carroll>But before we board that flight, a refresher. Jessica was one of the first women to publicly accuse Trump of sexual assault in 2016. It was on the front page of The New York Times.
<v E. Jean Carroll>但在我们登上那次航班之前，先复习一下。杰西卡是 2016 年第一批公开指控特朗普性侵犯的女性之一。那是在《纽约时报》的头版。

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:29.710
<v Jessica Leeds>I knew if the story did get any attention, that the first thing Trump would say is that I wasn't pretty enough.
<v Jessica Leeds>我知道如果这个故事引起任何关注，特朗普说的第一件事就是我不够漂亮。

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:30.780
<v E. Jean Carroll>Right.
<v E. Jean Carroll>对。

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:35.790
<v Jessica Leeds>I knew instinctively that that's what he was going to say.
<v Jessica Leeds>我本能地知道他会这么说。

00:08:35.790 --> 00:09:00.450
<v E. Jean Carroll>How did Jessica know? Because Jessica is an old bat. Old bats are the best, even better than screech owls. I'm an old bat myself. We old bats don't kid ourselves. And, in fact, one day after The New York Times published the story about what happened to Jessica on that plane in 1980, Trump yammered about her accusations at a rally.
<v E. Jean Carroll>杰西卡是怎么知道的？因为杰西卡是个老顽固。老顽固是最棒的，甚至比尖叫猫头鹰还要好。我自己也是个老顽固。我们这些老顽固不会自欺欺人。事实上，《纽约时报》报道了杰西卡 1980 年在飞机上发生的事情后的第二天，特朗普在一次集会上对她的指控大吵大闹。

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:22.590
<v Donald Trump>Oh, I was with Donald Trump in 1980. I was sitting with him on an airplane. And he went after me on the plane. Yeah, I'm going to go after-- believe me, she would not be my first choice, that I can tell you. You don't know. That would not be my first choice.
<v Donald Trump>哦，我 1980 年和唐纳德·特朗普在一起。我坐在飞机上和他在一起。他在飞机上对我动手动脚。是的，我要对……相信我，她不会是我的第一选择，我可以告诉你。你不知道。那不会是我的第一选择。

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.980
<v E. Jean Carroll>I told Jessica the same thing happened to me.
<v E. Jean Carroll>我告诉杰西卡同样的事情也发生在我身上。

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:47.180
<v E. Jean Carroll>I knew it was coming, too. I knew it, of course. And then he said it about me. She's not my type. Because they're running pictures of me when I'm 75 years old. And it's not fair. If they had run pictures of me as I looked-- you and I caught tons of shit because we're older women.
<v E. Jean Carroll>我也知道它要来了。我当然知道。然后他对我说了同样的话。她不是我的类型。因为他们放出了我 75 岁时的照片。这不公平。如果他们放出了我年轻时的照片——你和我都因为我们是年长的女性而受到了很多指责。

00:09:47.180 --> 00:10:18.250
<v Ira Glass>For the honor of Jessica Leeds and old bats everywhere, Jessica is a beauty now, and she was a beauty 40 years ago when she got on that flight to New York. And as she remembers it, it was a Braniff flight. Braniff was the chic airline. Her seat would have been full grain leather. Her flight attendant would have been clad in Halston. And her plane, it would have been painted in Perseus Green, Mercury Blue, or Sparkling Burgundy.
<v Ira Glass>为了杰西卡·利兹和所有地方的老顽固的荣誉，杰西卡现在是个美女，40 年前她登上那架飞往纽约的航班时也是个美女。正如她记忆中的那样，那是一次布兰尼夫航空的航班。她的座位应该是全粒面皮革的。她的空姐应该穿着哈尔斯顿设计的衣服。她的飞机，应该被漆成英仙座绿、水星蓝或闪亮勃艮第酒红。

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:21.850
<v E. Jean Carroll>And so what were you wearing? Do you remember, when you got on that plane?
<v E. Jean Carroll>那你当时穿什么？你还记得吗，当你登上那架飞机的时候？

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:41.630
<v Jessica Leeds>Yep. I had my best suit, was a brown tweed. Oh, I love that suit. And it was jacket and a skirt. It was a fabulous outfit. It really was. I never wore it again after that day, though. I hung on to it for quite a while, but I never wore it again.
<v Jessica Leeds>记得。我穿着我最好的西装，是棕色的粗花呢。哦，我爱那套西装。那是夹克和裙子。那是一套很棒的衣服。真的是。那天之后我就再也没穿过它了，虽然。我保留了它很长一段时间，但我再也没穿过它。

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:48.110
<v E. Jean Carroll>I know exactly what she means. It was the same for me with the dress I had on at Bergdorf's.
<v E. Jean Carroll>我完全明白她的意思。我和我在波道夫百货穿的那件衣服是一样的。

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:57.400
<v E. Jean Carroll>I didn't want to throw it away because it was a beautiful Donna Karan. I would never throw it away. But I couldn't put it on because bad things happened in that dress.
<v E. Jean Carroll>我不想把它扔掉，因为它是一件漂亮的唐娜·卡伦。我永远不会把它扔掉。但我不能穿它，因为那件衣服上发生了坏事。

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:58.970
<v Jessica Leeds>Yeah, exactly.
<v Jessica Leeds>是的，完全正确。

